(LST2-25 — out-of-bounds list element access sets the error flag and
 returns 0, and `On error` catches it. Probed at four boundaries the
 manual does not enumerate: far past the end, index 0, a negative index,
 and the first index past the end. All four behave identically.
 Also covers: LST2-26 (`On error` catches it), LST2-27 (the manual's
 worked bounds example), LST2-28 (a variable index out of range behaves
 the same as a literal one).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST2-25.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   element 100 set the error flag
   0
   element 0 set the error flag
   0
   element -1 set the error flag
   0
   element 4 set the error flag
   0
   a variable index out of range set the error flag
   0
)
a list called items is [11, 22, 33].
a number called past is element 100 of items.
On error print "element 100 set the error flag".
print past.
a number called zeroth is element 0 of items.
On error print "element 0 set the error flag".
print zeroth.
a number called back is element -1 of items.
On error print "element -1 set the error flag".
print back.
a number called edge is element 4 of items.
On error print "element 4 set the error flag".
print edge.
a number called spot is 9.
a number called found is element spot of items.
On error print "a variable index out of range set the error flag".
print found.
